@display("Contract")
contract: section {
  | The contract [layer](#heron.language.specifications.layers.layer) says exactly what a consumer can observe and rely on: the entries of the
  | product's [surfaces](#heron.tools.docs.design_system.surfaces), the errors they report, the types they exchange and examples of each.
  | It's still a requirement, never a design: it pins what a consumer relies on and nothing
  | inside. Every entry names the rules and use case steps it exists for in its constructor. An
  | error is a result, not an entry that solves anything: its prose links the rule that says when
  | it's reported, and it never names who reports it. The raiser names the error instead: an op's
  | or a command's `@throws`, a command holding it, or the subject of the rule it links.
  |
  | | Surface                     | Entry                                | Inputs                    | Outcomes                    |
  | | --------------------------- | ------------------------------------ | ------------------------- | --------------------------- |
  | | an API, `api`               | `op`                                 | params                    | responses, keyed by status  |
  | | a command line              | `command(name)`                      | params: arguments and `@option`s | exit statuses, as errors |
  | | an app, `screen(route)`     | `region`                             | what the reader enters    | what it shows, and errors   |
  | | a language, `std/grammar`   | `production`, `token`                | the text                  | the tree, and errors        |
  |
  | | Kind                                                   | Holds                                                         |
  | | ------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------- |
  | | `error(id)`, with `@status`, and `@severity` as a diagnostic | its `title`, with a `{name}` for each member, its members, `related`, a link to its rule, examples |
  | | `example(scenario)`                                     | one concrete case, exact inputs and outputs                    |
  | | `name(params): command(name)`                           | its errors, examples and subcommands                           |
  | | `screen(route)`                                        | regions, nested as the layout nests                            |

  surface_entry: term {{
    One entry of a surface, the same shape on every surface: a stable name, typed inputs,
    outcomes keyed by result, of which errors are some, named examples, a `deprecated` flag
    and its links. An `op`, a `command`, a `screen`'s region and a grammar's production are
    entries.
  }}

  error_kind: term {{
    `@status(404) name: error("id") { title = "…" }`, one problem the product reports, with
    one identity wherever it's reported: its id. Its `title` is the message, with a `{name}`
    for each value it carries filled in where it happens, each a member of the error typed as
    a record's is, as `payment: String`. Its prose links the rule that says when it's
    reported, as `Reported as [late_rejected] says.`; it never names who reports it, which
    comes from the rule's subject, the command holding it or a `@throws`. Its `@status` is its
    one required [projection](#language.specifications.contract.error_projection), and
    `@severity` says how serious it is as a diagnostic. These are core annotations, with no
    import.
  }}

  error_projection: term {{
    How one surface reports an error. Its `@status(code, text)`, the HTTP status, is the
    one every error has: the [derived codes](#language.specifications.contract.codes.derived_code)
    of the other [surfaces](#heron.tools.docs.design_system.surfaces) follow from it, its gRPC and [Sparrow code](#heron.language.apis.sparrow.status_codes.sparrow_code), its JSON-RPC code and its
    exit status, and an override annotation sets one where the [derived code](#heron.language.specifications.contract.codes.derived_code) doesn't fit:
    `@proto.status`, `@sparrow.status`, `@jsonrpc.code` or `@exit`. As a diagnostic it's
    reported with its `@severity` at its site, and on a screen at the field it's shown at.
  }}

  example_kind: term {{
    `example(scenario) {{ … }}`, a concrete case with its exact inputs and outputs,
    pinning only what the contract guarantees, the one kind of example: a rule's
    `example {{ … }}` child is a key or boundary case of that rule, and an op's sets the op's
    parameters as fields and its outcome as `returns` or `throws`.
  }}

  command_kind: term {{
    `name(params): command("heron check") { … }`, an entry of a command line, declared as an
    op is. Each parameter is an input: a positional argument in the order written, or, marked
    `@option(short, env, value)`, a named option, `--dry-run` for `dry_run`; `?` or a default
    makes it optional and `<variadic>` repeats it. Its body holds the errors it exits with, or
    `@throws` names them, its examples and its subcommands, and its usage line is derived from
    its parameters.
  }}

  screen_kind: term {{
    `name: screen(route = "/docs/<op>") { … }`, a view of an app and its regions, each
    region holding what it shows and the regions inside it.
  }}

  contract_layer: rule(system.checker) {
    must {{ give `error`, `example`, `command`, `screen`, `region`, `api`, `op` and the kinds of `std/types` and `std/grammar` the `CONTRACT` [layer](#language.specifications.layers.layer) }}
  }

  placeholders_declared: rule(system.checker) {
    if {{ an error's `title` or `related` holds a `{name}` that no member of the error declares, or a member appears in neither }}
    must {{ report it as an error at the [placeholder](#heron.language.core_format.expressions.placeholder), or as a warning at the member }}
    then {{ every value a message carries is also given as data, so nobody parses the message }}
  }

  error_title_required: rule(system.checker) {
    if {{ an error sets no `title` }}
    must {{ report the missing field, as for any member declared without a default }}
  }

  one_identity: rule(system.checker) {
    must {{ give each error one identity, its id, for every surface that reports it, and report it on each surface through its [projection](#language.specifications.contract.error_projection) there }}
    then {{ a diagnostic, an API error and an exit status of the same problem are one error }}
  }

  refines_scenario: rule(system.checker) {
    if {{ an example references something other than an outcome scenario }}
    must {{ report it as an error at the reference }}
  }

  command_params: rule(system.checker) {
    must {{ read a command's parameters as its inputs: each a positional argument, in the order written, unless `@option` marks it an option named `--` and its name with each `_` written `-`; optional where it has `?` or a default, the default its value when it's left out, and repeated where it's `<variadic>` }}
    example {{ `refund(payment: String, @option(short = "-n") dry_run?: Bool): command("shop refund")` takes the payment as its argument and `--dry-run` or `-n` as an option }}
  }

  usage_derived: rule {
    must {{ derive a command's usage line from its parameters: its name, then each option, as `[--name]` for a `Bool` and as `--name value` for any other, `value` the word its `@option(value)` gives or else its name, in brackets where it's optional, then each argument, as `<name>` and as `[name]` where it's optional, `…` after a variadic one's name, each by its [display name](#language.core_format.naming.display_name) or else its name with each `_` read as a space }}
    example {{ `check(@option json: Bool = false, entry_points<variadic>?: String): command("heron check")` reads `heron check [--json] [entry points…]` }}
  }

  command_view: rule(system.docs_generator) {
    must {{ show a command under its name as the page title, with its usage line, its arguments and its options each as a [table](#heron.language.markup.components.table) of name, type, default and description, or as lines where they're a [small table](#tools.docs.rendering.small_table), and the errors it exits with as a [table](#heron.language.markup.components.table) of exit status and title }}
  }

  screen_view: rule(system.docs_generator) {
    must {{ show a screen's route as a fact and its regions nested, each with an anchor }}
  }

  error_view: rule(system.docs_generator) {
    must {{ show an error as an entry of its section's list: its id and its title with each `{name}` marked on one line, its projections, its related message and, outside a command, who reports it, derived from the subject of a rule its prose links or from an op that throws it, as one line of facts, and its prose; and list it on the Errors page, grouped by id, with a link to its anchor }}
  }

  errors_on_surfaces: rule(system.docs_generator) {
    must {{ show on each [surface entry](#heron.language.specifications.contract.surface_entry) the errors it reports with their projection there: an op's with their status and their codes on its RPC bindings, a command's with their exit status, each code the effective one, derived or set by an override }}
  }

  error_granularity: rule(actors.author) {
    must {{ write two problems as one error when the same fix resolves both, and as two errors when their fixes differ }}
  }

  error_ids: rule(actors.author) {
    should {{ name a new error's id after its domain and its reason, as `refund.WINDOW_EXPIRED`, and keep an id once it's published }}
  }

  contract_minimal: rule(actors.author) {
    should {{ pin in an example only what a consumer may rely on, and leave out values the contract doesn't guarantee }}
  }

  codes: section {
    | An error's `@status` is its HTTP status, and the code it's reported with on every other
    | surface is derived from it by the rules below, so an error states one code and every
    | binding agrees. Where a [derived code](#heron.language.specifications.contract.codes.derived_code) doesn't fit, an override annotation of the surface
    | sets one: `@proto.status` for gRPC and Connect, `@sparrow.status` for Sparrow,
    | `@jsonrpc.code` for JSON-RPC and `@exit` for a command line.

    derived_code: term {{
      The code an error is reported with on a surface that doesn't speak HTTP, derived from
      its `@status`: its [gRPC code](#heron.language.apis.grpc.status_codes.grpc_code), its Sparrow core status code, its JSON-RPC code or its exit
      status. Its effective code on a surface is the derived one, or the one an override
      annotation sets.
    }}

    status_text: rule {
      must {{ read an error's `@status(code, text)` as its HTTP status and the text shown beside it, the error's id where `text` is left out }}
      example {{ `@status(404) no_such_file: error("server.NO_SUCH_FILE")` is shown as `404 server.NO_SUCH_FILE` }}
    }

    status_required: rule(system.checker) {
      if {{ an error has no `@status` }}
      must {{ report it as an error at the error }}
    }

    | | `@status`     | gRPC and Connect      | Sparrow               |
    | | ------------- | --------------------- | --------------------- |
    | | 400           | `INVALID_ARGUMENT`    | `invalid_argument`    |
    | | 401           | `UNAUTHENTICATED`     | `unauthenticated`     |
    | | 403           | `PERMISSION_DENIED`   | `permission_denied`   |
    | | 404           | `NOT_FOUND`           | `not_found`           |
    | | 409           | `ALREADY_EXISTS`      | `already_exists`      |
    | | 429           | `RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED`  | `resource_exhausted`  |
    | | 499           | `CANCELLED`           | `cancelled`           |
    | | 500           | `INTERNAL`            | `internal`            |
    | | 501           | `UNIMPLEMENTED`       | `unimplemented`       |
    | | 503           | `UNAVAILABLE`         | `unavailable`         |
    | | 504           | `DEADLINE_EXCEEDED`   | `deadline_exceeded`   |
    | | any other 4xx | `FAILED_PRECONDITION` | `failed_precondition` |
    | | any other 5xx | `INTERNAL`            | `internal`            |
    | | anything else | `UNKNOWN`             | `unknown`             |

    grpc_derived: rule {
      must {{ report an error over gRPC and Connect with the [gRPC code](#heron.language.apis.grpc.status_codes.grpc_code) the [table](#heron.language.markup.components.table) gives its `@status`, and over Sparrow with the core status code of the same row, unless its `@proto.status` or `@sparrow.status` sets one }}
      example {{ `@status(404)` is `NOT_FOUND` over gRPC and `not_found` over Sparrow, and `@status(422)` is `FAILED_PRECONDITION` and `failed_precondition` }}
    }

    | | `@status`     | JSON-RPC code                        |
    | | ------------- | ------------------------------------ |
    | | 400 to 499    | -32000 − (status − 400), as -32004 for 404 |
    | | 501           | -32601, method not found             |
    | | any other 5xx | -32603, internal error               |
    | | anything else | -32603, internal error               |

    jsonrpc_derived: rule {
      must {{ report an error over JSON-RPC with the code the [table](#heron.language.markup.components.table) gives its `@status`, unless its `@jsonrpc.code` sets one }}
      example {{ `@status(404)` is -32004, `@status(429)` is -32029 and `@status(500)` is -32603 }}
      then {{ a client error lands in the server range, -32000 to -32099, which JSON-RPC leaves to the server }}
    }

    | | `@status`     | Exit status |
    | | ------------- | ----------- |
    | | 400           | 2, a usage error |
    | | anything else | 1, a failure     |

    exit_derived: rule {
      must {{ end a command-line run that reports an error with the exit status the [table](#heron.language.markup.components.table) gives its `@status`, unless its `@exit` sets one }}
      example {{ `@status(400)` exits 2 and `@status(422)` exits 1 }}
    }

    redundant_override: rule(system.linter) {
      if {{ an override annotation, `@proto.status`, `@sparrow.status`, `@jsonrpc.code` or `@exit`, sets the code its error's `@status` derives }}
      should {{ report redundant code at the annotation }}
      then {{ a fix offered with it removes the annotation }}
    }

    effective_codes_shown: rule(system.docs_generator) {
      must {{ show an error's status with its text, and its effective code on each surface that reports it, as a fact marked derived or explicit }}
      example {{ an error with `@status(404)` thrown by an op bound to gRPC shows "gRPC `NOT_FOUND`, derived" }}
    }
  }

  errors: section {
    @status(422)
    @display("Placeholder not a member")
    placeholder_not_field: error("placeholder") {
      title = "{placeholder} in the title of {error} is no member of it; declare {placeholder} with its type, as {placeholder}: String"
      placeholder: String
      error: String
      | Reported as [placeholders_declared] says.
    }

    @status(422)
    @display("Member not in the message")
    @severity(warning)
    member_unused: error("placeholder") {
      title = "member {member} of {error} appears in neither its title nor its related message"
      member: String
      error: String
      | Reported as [placeholders_declared] says.
    }

    @status(422)
    @display("Error without a status")
    missing_status: error("status") {
      title = "error {error} has no @status; every error has one, which the codes of its other surfaces derive from"
      error: String
      | Reported as [codes.status_required] says.
    }

    @status(422)
    @display("Redundant code")
    @severity(warning)
    redundant_code: error("redundant-code") {
      title = "{annotation} sets {code}, the code {error}'s @status derives; leave it out"
      annotation: String
      code: String
      error: String
      | Reported as [codes.redundant_override] says.
    }

    @status(422)
    @display("Refines no outcome scenario")
    refines_not_scenario: error("reference") {
      title = "{name} is a quality scenario; an example pins down an outcome scenario"
      name: String
      | A reference to a node that's no scenario at all is reported as [layers.references.reference_kinds] says.
      | Reported as [refines_scenario] says.
    }
  }

  @display("An error on two surfaces")
  contract_example: example {
    | ```heron
    | import = ["std/doc@1", "std/types@1", "std/apis@1", "std/http@1"]
    |
    | desk: part {{ The support console. }}
    |
    | late_rejected: rule(desk) {
    |   if {{ a refund arrives more than 30 days after capture }}
    |   must {{ reject the request }}
    | }
    |
    | @status(422)
    | @exit(3)
    | window_expired: error("refund.WINDOW_EXPIRED") {
    |   title = "the refund window of payment {payment} closed on {closed}"
    |   payment: String
    |   closed: String
    |   | Reported as [late_rejected] says.
    | }
    |
    | @throws(window_expired)
    | refund(payment: String, @option(short = "-n") dry_run?: Bool): command("shop refund", late_rejected)
    | ```
  }
}
